The BIOS's check for a key (INT 16h, AH = 01h and 11h) returned with the interrupt flag as its caller had pushed
it: SeaBIOS returns from its services by IRET. The PC's and the AT's BIOS turn interrupts on when they are entered
and return from these two with RET 2, the flags being the answer (ZF): the caller gets them back enabled, whatever
it came with. DOS's power manager counts on that. It takes INT 16h, is entered with interrupts off as any
interrupt handler, calls the BIOS (PUSHF, CALL FAR), and returns to the program with the flags the BIOS gave it
(PUSHF ... POPF, RETF 2): with SeaBIOS, interrupts off. A program that asks for a key and then waits for a timer
tick by HLT stops there for good: a DOS benchmark in its continuous mode, after its first score (the same in QEMU
with its SeaBIOS). Now the two functions set IF in the flags they return. (vendor/bios.bin is built with it:
tools/build_seabios.sh.)

diff --git a/seabios/patch b/seabios/patch
--- a/seabios/patch
+++ b/seabios/patch
@@ -210,6 +210,28 @@
  }
  
  // Write a character to the special debugging port.
+diff --git a/src/kbd.c b/src/kbd.c
+index 15e5ae78..9da583c4 100644
+--- a/src/kbd.c
++++ b/src/kbd.c
+@@ -124,6 +124,9 @@ static void
+ handle_1601(struct bregs *regs)
+ {
+     dequeue_key(regs, 0, 0);
++    // (tiny386) As the PC's and the AT's BIOS: it returns with interrupts on (STI on entry, RET 2 for the flags).
++    // A caller may count on it: DOS's power manager passes the flags it gets back on to the program.
++    regs->flags |= F_IF;
+ }
+ 
+ // get shift flag status
+@@ -183,6 +186,7 @@ static void
+ handle_1611(struct bregs *regs)
+ {
+     dequeue_key(regs, 0, 1);
++    regs->flags |= F_IF;   // (tiny386: as handle_1601)
+ }
+ 
+ // get extended keyboard status
 diff --git a/src/misc.c b/src/misc.c
 index b5117304..9cdc6d08 100644
 --- a/src/misc.c
